class Solution {
  public int[] frequencyFilesProofStrip(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Map<Integer,Integer>counts=new TreeMap<>();for(int x:a)counts.merge(x,1,Integer::sum);out.addAll(counts.values());}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build counts

Count how many times each different number appears in `readings`. Sort the different numbers from smallest to largest. Put their counts in that order in `counts`. For [5, 2, 5], the numbers are 2 and 5, so `counts` is [1, 2].

Return the answer

Complete `frequencyFilesProofStrip` so it returns `counts`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `counts` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + d log d); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length; d is the number of different values. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

